# Migrations

- Generate with `bin/rails g migration descriptive_name` so filename starts with valid **`YYYYMMDDHHMMSS`** (14-digit decimal version in `schema_migrations`). Hand-made files: never shorten, reuse, collide, or use non-digits in that prefix.
- Prefer reversible **`change`** over `up`/`down`.
- Required columns: `null: false` aligned with model `validates … presence` (multi-step add when table has rows — see below).
- DB defaults where they belong at rest layer.
- Index every FK and hot filter column; **unique** index for every `validates :uniqueness`.
- `t.references …, foreign_key: true` (or explicit `add_reference` + FK).
- **Never** edit a migration that has already shipped to an environment — add a new file.
- **One** logical schema change per migration file.
- Postgres large-table indexes: `disable_ddl_transaction!` + `add_index …, algorithm: :concurrently`.
- Backfilling `null: false` on populated column: (1) add column with default / backfill, (2) `change_column_null`, (3) remove temporary default if any — never single reckless `change_column_null` on unknown-null data.
- Heavy backfills: job or follow-up migration — not `update_all` on huge hot table inside blocking migration transaction.
- Dropping column: deploy 1 — `self.ignored_columns += [:col]` on model; deploy 2 — `remove_column`.
- FKs: `on_delete: :restrict` (or stricter team standard — default here restrict).
- Obey `strong_migrations` when gem present.
- Index name > 63 chars (Postgres): pass explicit `name:`.
- Bulk seed in migration: `insert_all` / `upsert_all` — not `rows.each(&:save!)`.
